so i'm here with julia quirianova the  ceo of the ukrainian investment group  smart holding  miss kirianova  your  investment group is in charge of various  key sectors within ukraine including  metals  the energy industry and agriculture as  well so how much has  the current war in ukraine affected the  running of your business  well thank you for the question and nice  meeting you uh indeed the war uh  has a very significant  impact towards our business  all the industries were down each uh  separately because of different factors  for example  metals and mining we expect to produce  only one-thirds and export one-thirds of  the production that we used to  agriculture is also more than 50 percent  will suffer from the war but the key  problem to the country now is logistics  because you know it's like  vines or it's like  blood vessels that deliver your blood  and they are uh  really destroyed now therefore we have  leakages like stolen grains by russian  troops or gems or tromboth if you  compare to blood vessels  these are  shortages with the expo channels because  black sea and azov ports are blocked  and our railway doesn't allow for  exports so we're losing like 50 percent  of our production because of the war  you were just talking about you know the  vital elements of business you have over  100 000 employees who work for your  investment group  how has it been managing  your employees in this really dramatic  time for your country  well  when  you know business is about people  so it's always a tragedy when you see  something like in ukraine happening  so first day we tried  only safety measures that's all we can  do we tried to evacuate people  as many as we can from hotspots  we organized offices in  western ukraine  and  all the processes all the chains of  communications were broken  well because of  pandemic times we have very good online  systems  and therefore  we had the means to communicate but  people were not able to communicate  they were stuck  in the situation so it took us time  to make the organization feel  a bit more alive and i started from  myself  to tell the truth so you know i was kind  of paralyzed a few days after the war  started  and then i did two things  the first one  i  just accepted  what happened and said okay  i have nothing  at all  i have no home i have nothing  no work for example i'm out of my job  and then  i realized i have myself  and that's what really  made me  start  over uh start again and come over of  everything so then we try to  do whatever we can for our employees and  to give them job and to pay them  salaries  speaking of the destruction that the war  has caused two of your uh steel plants  in mariupol were actually just destroyed  by russian  shelling including the azov style steel  plant which you know has been in the  headlines  um so much  how do you deal with the emotional shock  of that kind of thing  and how do you get over it from a  business perspective as well  we cannot  kind of deal with it in any way we just  we just have to live  to  to help  as much as we can  and to  make people  feel  that  we take care of them and  i don't know you see the the point is  that none of the businesses was prepared  we were preparing ourselves but it was  not like what we expected the scale of  this invasion the cruelty and everything  so you cannot just 100 000 people move  somewhere  when the steel mills are there you  cannot and to stop operations on time is  a very judgmental issue  you know and also the operat the some  production process needs certain time to  be stopped so all of these factor  factors  could not allow for perfect efficiency  in saving  lives  but most of our people  are  out of marijuana  we still have  some missing people  and that will be with us  forever now here at the world economic  forum uh there's been a lot of talk  about the rebuilding of ukraine  including you know in president  ukrainian president vladimir zelinsky's  opening address 